# /etc/stayconnect/pmsd.env — the DARK environment for stayconnect-pmsd.service.
#
# THIS FILE IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY OF SETTINGS, and that is the whole point.
#
# `EnvironmentFile=` in the unit is not optional, so the file must exist or systemd refuses to start the
# service. What it must NOT do is carry configuration the dark daemon has no business having.
#
# With no Phase-3 flag variable set, iamv2.LoadPMSConfigFromEnv resolves every flag to OFF, and
# pmsd.Run returns immediately: no assignment is loaded, no database pool is constructed, no secret is
# decrypted, no worker starts and no PMS socket is opened. The process logs that it has nothing to do and
# exits 0. `Restart=on-failure` (not `always`) means a clean exit is a clean exit, so DARK never restart-loops.
#
# The flags are ABSENT rather than written as `=false` on purpose: the deployment gate proves darkness by
# asserting that no Phase-3 flag name appears in any env file or unit on the appliance, and a file that spells
# them out — even set to false — would weaken that proof into "somebody read the values correctly".
#
# TURNING THE CONNECTOR ON IS A SEPARATE, EXPLICITLY AUTHORIZED STEP, and this file is not where it starts.
# The variables it requires — the Phase-3 master switch and its connector/ingest surfaces, the least-privilege
# site-database DSN, the signed-assignment path and control-plane verification key, and the interface-secret,
# evidence and event-identity keyrings — are listed in the Phase-3 deployment runbook and in
# internal/iamv2/pms_config.go.
#
# THEIR NAMES ARE DELIBERATELY NOT SPELLED OUT HERE. The darkness proof this project relies on is a plain
# search for a Phase-3 flag name across /etc/stayconnect: if no env file mentions one, nothing can have set
# one. Writing the names into a comment would turn that clean, robust check into three false alarms on a
# security-critical surface, and a check that cries wolf is a check people learn to skip.
#
# None of those settings is installed by scripts/install-pmsd-dark.sh.
